  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nToday through next Monday... The primary focus during this \nforecast period currently revolves around a \"limited\" concern for \nfire danger on Thursday, some isolated to scattered rain chances \ntoday as well as Sunday, and the potential for at to below \nfreezing low temperatures Friday night through Sunday night. \n\nConditions will remain warm today ahead of a cold front that will\nmove through the region tonight into early Thursday morning.Ahead\nof this front, showers are spreading northeastward from central\nand southern Louisiana and will bring rain chances (50-60%) to\nsouthern Mississippi with isolated chances (<20%) elsewhere.\nAreas of the Pinebelt could see up to 0.25\" of total rainfall \nwith the the majority of the forecast area remaining dry.\n\nBehind this front on Thursday, noticeably colder, drier air will \nadvect into the region.  Dew points through the day will gradually \nfall into the mid to lower 40s F, with some upper 30s F possible \nas well. Meanwhile, highs will range from the middle 60s F north \nto middle 70s F south. With afternoon relative humidity values \ngenerally ranging between 35-45%, northwest winds sustained around\n15 mph with gusts around 25 mph, and with the extremely dry soil \nconditions and vegetation across the area, a \"limited\" threat for \nfire danger will exist across the entire CWA.\n\nColder, dry weather will persist into the upcoming weekend and \nthe beginning of next week. High temperatures Friday through \nMonday will be noticeably cooler, middle 50s F to middle 60s F.\nOvernight lows will see multiple opportunities to fall below zero \nparticularly in northeastern and east-central Mississippi where \nlows Friday night and Saturday night likely drop into the upper \n20s F. Lows elsewhere will generally be in the 30s F. A forecast \ngraphic highlighting the areas most likely to be impacted by these\nfreezing and sub-freezing temperatures will be delineated by a \nconcurrent \"Limited\" HWO product.\n\nModel guidance consensus grows concerning the potential for a wave\nof low pressure to develop along the northern Gulf coast into\nearly next week. Rain chances have substantially trended up and it\ncurrently appears that most areas could see some much needed\nrainfall Sunday night into Monday. Overnight lows will likely be\npredicated on exactly how that disturbance evolves with the\ncurrently forecast lows near freezing in the Golden Triangle area\nlikely warmer should some of the faster/wetter guidance prove \nmore correct. The key takeaway here is that despite the near \nfreezing forecast temperatures coupled with 20-40% rainfall \nchances late Sunday night into early Monday morning, it's more \nlikely that one of these regimes proves more accurate and winter \nweather is not expected with this activity. Either it will be \nwarmer and wetter or remain dry and chilly. We'll have a better \nidea of which of these two regimes we'll get as we get closer.\n/86/19/",
